{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"5","reason":"The inverse identity log_a(a^k) = k gives log_2(2^5) = 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h048_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cnm_h048_q1_m1","label":"Apply the inverse identity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_h048_q1_m1":["cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"16","reason":"Using a^(log_a u) = u for u > 0 gives 2^(log_2 16) = 16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h048_q2","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cnm_h048_q2_m1","label":"Apply the inverse identity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_h048_q2_m1":["cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 7","reason":"By the inverse identity a^(log_a t)=t for t>0, 10^(log_10 7)=7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q34","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043"],"id":"logarithm-definition","label":"Use the meaning of a logarithm","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithm-definition":["cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: the value is a and depends only on a","reason":"By the definition of log_b(a), b raised to that exponent equals a.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043"],"id":"apply-logarithm-definition","label":"Read the logarithm as an exponent","methodDirectNodeIds":{"apply-logarithm-definition":["cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: x=10 or x=100","reason":"Let t=log_10(x), with x>0. Taking logarithms gives t^2=3t-2, so t=1 or 2. Therefore x=10 or 100, and both satisfy the original equation.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h046"],"id":"take-logarithms-and-factor","label":"Take logarithms and factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"take-logarithms-and-factor":["cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_224","cnm_h043","cnm_h046"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. 1/2","reason":"The left side is 9, so 8x+5=9 and x=4/8=1/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043"],"id":"logarithm-definition","label":"Use the meaning of the logarithm","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithm-definition":["cnm_h048","cnm_p145"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048","cnm_p145","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 597","reason":"Writing t=log_b(a) gives t^2017=2017t, whose real solutions are 0 and ±2017^(1/2016). Each of the 199 bases gives three distinct positive values a=b^t, so there are 3·199=597 ordered pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"logarithm-substitution","label":"Substitute for the logarithm","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithm-substitution":["cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, log_b a","reason":"Let t=log_b a>0. Then log_b(a^p)=p·t=log_b t, so a^p=t=log_b a.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1982_AMC_12_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h046"],"id":"replace-inner-logarithm","label":"Use the logarithm as an exponent","methodDirectNodeIds":{"replace-inner-logarithm":["cnm_h048","math:real-exponent-laws"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h043","cnm_h046"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2015","reason":"Let t = log_5(2015). Since 625 = 5^4 and all bases are positive, (625^t)^(1/4) = 5^t = 2015.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_12B_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h039"],"id":"rewrite-as-powers-of-five","label":"Rewrite 625 using base 5","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rewrite-as-powers-of-five":["cnm_h048","math:real-exponent-laws"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h043","cnm_h039"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 13, with n = 256","reason":"Let t=log₄(n)>0. Then log₁₆(n)=t/2, so log₂(t)-1=(1/2)log₂(t). Hence log₂(t)=2, t=4, and n=4⁴=256. Its digit sum is 2+5+6=13.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12A_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_042"],"id":"change-to-base-two","label":"Reduce to a linear logarithmic equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"change-to-base-two":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_h028"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_042","cnm_h048","cnm_h028"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"exponentiate-outer-logs","label":"Obtain a quadratic in the inner logarithm","methodDirectNodeIds":{"exponentiate-outer-logs":["cnm_h043","cnm_h048","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h048","cnm_224"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"remove-outer-logarithms","label":"Remove the outer logarithms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-outer-logarithms":["cnm_h045","cnm_h048","cnm_h028","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h048","cnm_h028","cnm_224"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 1/6","reason":"Put u=log_b 2, v=log_b 3, and t=log_b x. Taking log_b gives u(u+t)=v(v+t). Since u≠v, t=-(u+v)=-log_b 6, hence x=1/6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h046","cnm_040"],"id":"take-logarithms-and-factor","label":"Turn the powers into products","methodDirectNodeIds":{"take-logarithms-and-factor":["cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_152","cnm_151"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_152","cnm_151","cnm_h043","cnm_h046","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2q-p-r","reason":"The given equations imply log a=p log x, log b=q log x, and log c=r log x. Hence log(b²/(ac))=(2q-p-r)log x=y log x. Since log x≠0, y=2q-p-r.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"convert-logarithms-to-powers","label":"Express a, b, and c as powers of x","methodDirectNodeIds":{"convert-logarithms-to-powers":["cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 8/7","reason":"Dividing 7^(x+7)=8^x by 7^x gives 7^7=(8/7)^x. By the definition of logarithm, x=log_(8/7)(7^7), so b=8/7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"exponential-inverse","label":"Rewrite and invert the exponential","methodDirectNodeIds":{"exponential-inverse":["cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2 real values","reason":"Set t=log_b x. Then log_a x=t log_a b, so t^2=1 because log_a b≠0. Thus x=b or 1/b, both positive and distinct since b≠1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q31","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"logarithmic-square-branches","label":"Take the two square-root branches","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithmic-square-branches":["cnm_220","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_220","cnm_h048"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-logarithmic-square","label":"Factor the difference of squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-logarithmic-square":["cnm_152","cnm_224","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_152","cnm_224","cnm_h048"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h046","cnm_h043","cnm_074"],"id":"common-base-square","label":"Use one logarithm base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-base-square":["cnm_h045","cnm_220","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_220","cnm_h048","cnm_h046","cnm_h043","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. All positive x≠1","reason":"For x>0 and x≠1, change of base gives (ln x/ln 3)(ln 5/ln x)=ln 5/ln 3=log₃5. The excluded value x=1 cannot be a logarithm base.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"use-inverse-exponents","label":"Use logarithms as exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-inverse-exponents":["cnm_h048","cnm_h041"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048","cnm_h041"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, (1+√5)/2","reason":"If the common logarithm value is t, then p = 9^t, q = 12^t, and p+q = 16^t. Since 12² = 9×16, q² = p(p+q). Therefore r = q/p > 0 satisfies r² = 1+r, whose positive root is (1+√5)/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1988_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"introduce-common-exponent","label":"Common exponent","methodDirectNodeIds":{"introduce-common-exponent":["cnm_h048","cnm_222"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048","cnm_222"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (270)","reason":"Equal differences of logarithms give a common ratio r between successive arguments. Thus r^4=1250/162=625/81=(5/3)^4. Since r is positive, r=5/3 and x=162(5/3)=270.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"interpolate-logarithms","label":"Interpolate the logarithms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"interpolate-logarithms":["cnm_h202","cnm_h044","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h202","cnm_h044","cnm_h048"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h201","cnm_h043","cnm_h046"],"id":"equal-logarithmic-steps","label":"Find the common logarithmic difference","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-logarithmic-steps":["cnm_h202","cnm_h044","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h202","cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_h201","cnm_h043","cnm_h046"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 3 + √2","reason":"The first equation gives a·2^b = 64, so log₂a = 6 - b. Logging the second gives b log₂a = 7, hence b(6 - b) = 7. Both roots b = 3 ± √2 satisfy the original constraints, and the larger is 3 + √2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"logarithmic-sum-and-product","label":"Turn logarithms into a sum and product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithmic-sum-and-product":["cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_221"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_221"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. y = x","reason":"If (u,v) lies on y = 2^x, then v = 2^u and (v,u) lies on y = log_2 x. Swapping coordinates is reflection in y = x.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h048_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:inverse-functions"],"id":"inverse-function-symmetry","label":"Use inverse-function symmetry","methodDirectNodeIds":{"inverse-function-symmetry":["cnm_h048","cnm_131"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048","cnm_131","math:inverse-functions"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, x=3","reason":"The equation becomes 10^(5x)=10^15. Since the exponential function with base 10 is strictly increasing, 5x=15 and x=3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_10A_Q02","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"base-ten-logarithms","label":"Take base-10 logarithms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"base-ten-logarithms":["cnm_h044","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h048"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 31, with x = 4/27","reason":"Change of base gives 2 ln(2x) = 3 ln(3x), hence ln x = 2 ln 2 - 3 ln 3 = ln(4/27). Therefore x = 4/27, which satisfies the stated domain restrictions, and p + q = 4 + 27 = 31.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"use-a-common-exponent","label":"Use the common logarithm as an exponent","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-a-common-exponent":["cnm_h048","cnm_h042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048","cnm_h042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (22)","reason":"The n=5 identity gives b=2 and k·5^a=10^4. The m=25 identity gives a=4 and k·25^b=10^4. Hence k=10000/625=16, so k+a+b=16+4+2=22.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"exponentiate-and-compare-powers","label":"Exponentiate and compare powers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"exponentiate-and-compare-powers":["cnm_h048","cnm_h040"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048","cnm_h040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"3","reason":"f^(-1)(8) = log_2 8 = 3, since f(3) = 2^3 = 8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h048_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_020"],"id":"cnm_h048_q3_m1","label":"Evaluate the logarithmic inverse","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_h048_q3_m1":["cnm_h048","cnm_h043"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h048","cnm_h043","cnm_020"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (271)","reason":"The outer argument must be positive, which successively requires log_{1/4}(log_16(log_{1/16} x)) > 1, then 0 < log_16(log_{1/16} x) < 1/4, and finally 1 < log_{1/16} x < 2. Thus the domain is (1/256, 1/16), whose length is 15/256, giving m+n=271.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h046","cnm_h043"],"id":"outside-in-domain","label":"Work inward from the outer logarithm","methodDirectNodeIds":{"outside-in-domain":["cnm_h028","cnm_h047","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h028","cnm_h047","cnm_h048","cnm_h046","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2^16","reason":"Logarithm laws give e^p = ∏_{k=1}^6 k^k. Only 2^2, 4^4, and 6^6 contribute factors of 2, with exponents 2, 8, and 6 respectively. Their sum is 16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12B_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_142","cnm_143"],"id":"factor-exponential","label":"Convert logarithms into a product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-exponential":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_h044","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_142","cnm_143"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3","reason":"Since 100=10² and 1000=10³, the equation becomes 10^(5x)=10^15. Injectivity gives 5x=15, hence x=3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12A_Q02","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"take-logarithms","label":"Take logarithms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"take-logarithms":["cnm_h043","cnm_h044","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h044","cnm_h048"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 16,089","reason":"The composition is h₁(x)=10x-1. Starting from 1 gives 9,89,889,... . In general hₙ(1)=(8·10ⁿ+1)/9, which has n-1 digits equal to 8 followed by 9. The requested digit sum is 2010·8+9=16,089.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h048","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h197"],"id":"solution:decimal-pattern:22429cdf3c6a","label":"Track how the digits change","methodDirectNodeIds":{"decimal-pattern":["math:function-composition-iteration","cnm_h200","cnm_030"]},"nodeIds":["math:function-composition-iteration","cnm_h200","cnm_030","cnm_h048","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h197"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, smaller than any specified positive number","reason":"The expression equals log_10(1+3/x^2), which lies between 0 and log_10(31/4)<1. For any ε>0, choosing x sufficiently large that 3/x^2<10^ε-1 makes the expression less than ε.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h046","cnm_h042","cnm_096"],"id":"combine-and-bound-logarithms","label":"Combine the logarithms and choose x","methodDirectNodeIds":{"combine-and-bound-logarithms":["cnm_h044","cnm_h047","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h047","cnm_h048","cnm_h043","cnm_h046","cnm_h042","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 1+log(3)/log(2); the full solution set is {1, log_2(6)}.","reason":"With u=2^x, the equation becomes (u-2)(u-6)=0. Thus x=1 or x=log_2(6)=1+log(3)/log(2), so D is a solution.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1969_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h043"],"id":"quadratic-exponential-substitution","label":"Substitute for the repeated power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadratic-exponential-substitution":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_224","cnm_h048","cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_224","cnm_h048","cnm_h045","cnm_h044","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (1)","reason":"For x=j+t with j=1,...,2013 and 0≤t<1, the condition is j(2014^t-1)≤1. Thus the allowed interval has length log_2014((j+1)/j). Their sum is log_2014(∏_{j=1}^{2013}(j+1)/j)=log_2014(2014)=1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h046"],"id":"unit-intervals-and-telescoping","label":"Fix the floor on each unit interval","methodDirectNodeIds":{"unit-intervals-and-telescoping":["math:floor-ceiling","math:telescoping","cnm_h042","cnm_h044","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["math:floor-ceiling","math:telescoping","cnm_h042","cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_h043","cnm_h046"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, x=576","reason":"Positive edge lengths require x>1. Dividing abc=2(ab+ac+bc) by 2abc gives 1/2=1/a+1/b+1/c=log_x 2+log_x 3+log_x 4=log_x 24. Therefore √x=24, so x=576.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_042","cnm_h048"],"id":"common-base-parameter","label":"Common-base parameter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-base-parameter":["cnm_p161","cnm_p163","cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h043"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_042","cnm_h048","cnm_p161","cnm_p163","cnm_h044","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 2013","reason":"For n=2009, let t=T(n), s=T(n-1), and u=T(n-2). Then log₂log₂B=st+u, which lies strictly between t and 2^t. Thus T(2011)<B<T(2012). After 2011 logarithms the result lies in (1,2), after 2012 in (0,1), and after 2013 it is negative. The next logarithm is undefined, so k=2013.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h200","cnm_h040"],"id":"bound-between-consecutive-towers","label":"Compare with consecutive towers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-between-consecutive-towers":["cnm_h046","math:function-composition-iteration","cnm_h048","cnm_h047","cnm_h214"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h046","math:function-composition-iteration","cnm_h048","cnm_h047","cnm_h214","cnm_h044","cnm_h200","cnm_h040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. Infinitely many","reason":"For a logarithm base b > 1, each x = b^(-π/2 - kπ), with k = 0, 1, 2, ..., lies in (0, 1) and satisfies cos(log_b x) = 0. These are infinitely many distinct zeros. A base between 0 and 1 also gives infinitely many.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1999_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h046","cnm_h057","cnm_h060","cnm_h042"],"id":"construct-all-zeros","label":"Convert cosine zeros into inputs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"construct-all-zeros":["cnm_h049","math:infinitude-by-construction","cnm_h047","cnm_h048","cnm_h068"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h049","math:infinitude-by-construction","cnm_h047","cnm_h048","cnm_h068","cnm_h046","cnm_h057","cnm_h060","cnm_h042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 12","reason":"Set t=ln x. The quadratic is 8t²-(7ln m+6ln n)t-2013ln m ln n=0 and has two real roots. Hence the product K of the x-solutions satisfies K⁸=m⁷n⁶. For any prime dividing m, its exponents obey 7u+6v=8w with u>0; the smallest possible w is 4, attained at (u,v)=(2,3). Therefore K≥2⁴=16. Equality forces m=4 and n=8, so m+n=12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h046","cnm_h048","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"],"id":"logarithmic-root-sum-and-prime-exponents","label":"Use the sum of logarithmic roots and prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithmic-root-sum-and-prime-exponents":["cnm_h045","cnm_226","cnm_223","math:prime-factorization","math:extremal-principle"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_226","cnm_223","math:prime-factorization","math:extremal-principle","cnm_h044","cnm_h046","cnm_h048","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"cnm_h048","